The near-thermal radiation emitted by a compact object formed from collapsed matter whose gravity prevents anything, including light, from escaping beyond a certain boundary is a predicted output of combining quantum principles with gravitational collapse: it takes as input the mass (and other bulk properties) of the object and yields as output a characteristic radiation spectrum and an associated temperature/energy loss rate, asserting that such objects slowly lose mass by emission even though classically nothing should escape. It applies specifically to such collapsed objects once quantum effects near their boundary are taken into account, and holds despite the original derivation's reliance on questionable extremely-high-frequency assumptions, since alternative derivations reproduce the same result without depending on those assumptions.
